Mental Health & Substance Use
Depressed mood, Anhedonia, Pervasive fatigue

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MHS-033
Reason for Visit: Acute refusal of food and fluids for 48 hours, profound psychomotor retardation, and emerging passive suicidal ideation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult child
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute deterioration in a known depressive episode, characterized by a complete cessation of oral intake, inability to rise from bed, and statements of wishing to die over the past 48 hours. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically mandated to evaluate hydration status, assess immediate suicide risk, and determine if involuntary psychiatric admission was required for life-saving intervention.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have no energy to chew or swallow. Everything is gray. I just want to go to sleep and not wake up."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a persistent pervasive depressed mood lasting for the past three months, severely worsening over the last week. The patient exhibits total anhedonia and loss of interest in all previously enjoyed activities. The adult child reports significant involuntary weight loss (approx. 5 kg) over the last month. The patient experiences insomnia nearly every day and exhibits profound psychomotor retardation observable by others. There is a pervasive fatigue and total loss of energy, culminating in the current refusal to eat or drink. The patient expresses deep feelings of worthlessness and excessive inappropriate guilt regarding their perceived burden on the family. There is a diminished ability to think or concentrate. The patient confirms recurrent thoughts of death and passive suicidal ideation ("wishing I wouldn't wake up"), but denies an active plan or intent to self-harm.
Functional Status: Completely bedbound; dependent for all basic ADLs due to severe psychomotor retardation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration and starvation; moderate risk of suicide; severe caregiver burnout.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.4 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 58 Kg
General: Cachectic appearance, flat affect, minimal eye contact, profoundly slow speech, dry lips.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals mild resting tachycardia but normal rhythm. Capillary refill is sluggish (~3 seconds). Oral mucosa is dry, indicating mild to moderate dehydration. Abdomen is scaphoid but soft and non-tender. Neurological exam shows intact gross motor function but immense effort required to move. Mental state examination is dominated by severe depressed mood, total anhedonia, pervasive fatigue, and passive suicidal ideation without psychotic features (no delusions or hallucinations).
Environmental Observations: Curtains drawn, room completely dark. Untouched meals left on a bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Prescribed Fluoxetine 20mg; adult child reports the patient stopped taking it two weeks ago due to "lack of energy to swallow the pill."

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Major depressive disorder - Status: Exacerbated (Severe melancholic features, medication non-compliance)
Differentials:

Hypothyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely to be the sole cause of such profound, rapid-onset melancholia and suicidality, though thyroid function tests are mandatory outpatient.

Bipolar Disorder (Depressive Phase) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of any history of manic or hypomanic episodes.

Dementia (e.g., Pseudodementia) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's intact orientation and ability to answer questions accurately when given sufficient time (psychomotor slowing rather than true cognitive loss).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe melancholic depression resulting in a life-threatening cessation of oral intake and psychomotor collapse. Telephone triage correctly identified the risk of dehydration and starvation. Urgent after-hours assessment was absolutely critical to physically assess the degree of dehydration, evaluate the immediate lethality of the suicidal ideation, and establish a safe plan of care. While the patient is severely ill, clinical assessment deemed the dehydration mild-moderate and the suicidality passive. Therefore, aggressive home-based intervention (supervised oral rehydration and medication restart) was initiated to prevent an involuntary hospital admission, which would have been unavoidable without this urgent in-person medical review.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Directly supervised the consumption of 500ml of oral rehydration solution during the visit. Re-administered the patient's prescribed Fluoxetine 20mg. Established a strict agreement with the adult child to provide continuous supervision for the next 24 hours. Expected investigation results (outpatient): standardized depression rating scales demonstrating severe depressive symptoms.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Directed primarily at the caregiver): Implement "Micro-Goal Feeding." Do not present full meals. Present one single bite of high-calorie food (e.g., a spoon of peanut butter) or one sip of water every hour. Reward the patient with quiet, non-demanding presence upon swallowing. This reduces the overwhelming executive function required to eat a meal and utilizes the caregiver's presence as gentle operant reinforcement for basic survival tasks.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral generated for the community mental health Crisis Assessment and Treatment Team (CATT) for daily home visits.
DME/Safety (Optional): Nil.
Follow-up: CATT team to review tomorrow; GP review mandated within 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient completely refuses all fluids for another 12 hours, if they become unrousable, or if active suicidal planning emerges.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute stabilization of hydration provided; severe depression is life-threatening and requires intensive monitoring.
Clinical Course: The psychomotor retardation will slowly lift as nutrition is restored and the antidepressant reaches therapeutic levels again (2-4 weeks).

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Caregiver: If the patient refuses all fluids tomorrow, or if you feel you cannot keep them safe from self-harm, you must call an ambulance for an emergency hospital admission.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain is currently starved of the chemicals needed to produce energy and hope. Stopping the medication made this crash much worse. We must restart the medication and slowly give your body fuel through tiny sips of water.
Actionable Steps: Swallow the small sips of water when your child offers them. Allow your child to administer your medication daily. You do not have to solve anything today; just focus on swallowing.

